Willie Harold how well-known is his  story and what unique angle does he give  you into the film not only for who he  was but the way you're gonna follow him  and not his victims it's not a  well-known story even in the MC lund in  the north of Germany it's not that  well-known you know a generation before  mine still kind of remembers but it's  not a case that has been talked about or  written about a lot I I was looking for  a story that would enable me to you know  sort of look at the dynamic structure of  National Socialism because we have  certain national myths that say the V  Moffitt was clean they were not involved  in atrocities they were not  ideologically driven they didn't partake  in massacres of course that that's all  not true as we found out in the late 80s  but there are other national myths that  are still kind of alive and we ran  straight into them when we were looking  for government funding because a lot of  people were trying to block our path and  and kind of burry the film and not  having have it funded because they felt  the film was amoral and that it you know  besmirched or soil to Vermont amoral  because the approach was clearly one  where we were telling the story from the  point of view of a perpetrator and that  was kind of a there's only two films in  the entire film history of Germany since  1945 that are told from the perspective  of the perpetrator which is death is my  business which is I think 1984 it's it's  a fictional biography of food or hosts  ironically of course written by a  Frenchman if Germans wouldn't write it  and 1977 was the van's a conference  which is of course where the final  solution was negotiated and agreed upon  those perpetrators however our primary  architects of the system and and making  a film about the dynamic structure to me  involve making a film about the back row  of perpetrators about the people who  were the boots on the ground who kept  the system running for whatever  you know not always ideological and and  so I came across the story and I  realized this story would actually allow  me to do a bit of an allow system  analysis because it doesn't just involve  the very bottom you know of the ordinary  soldier but it goes all the way up to  the top in the court scene where you  actually meet you know the generals and  the actual architects of that of that of  that system but when you're telling a  story from a perpetrators point of view  you have to make sure that you honor the  victims of that perpetrator so how does  that reveal itself in the way that you  and your cinematographer Florian Bauhaus  shot the film what was important  visually from what was committed in  terms of how you wanted to represent  something that actually occurred but  also honor the victims who were targeted  by him  we actually dialed back what they really  did I mean we they threw hand grenades  into that pit and you know body parts  were flying around so we we we kind of  drew a line and at a certain point and  said okay this is not something we want  to we want to show we had to show a  little bit because I have to I think you  have to understand what it means to get  shot with a flak I mean it's not the  best tool for you know murdering people  in a pit and I think if you don't show  any violence at all if you don't show  the brutality of what is being done to  the to the prisoners  you also betray the victims so so my  first instinct was to not show very much  at all and then I realized but you know  it'll it doesn't mean anything you don't  feel what happens to these people and  Florian was insistent that we would also  always for every scene with the  prisoners do these very long pans  because we wanted even though we we  decided not to have a character we  identify with on the other side of the  fence we wanted to perceive them as as  as human beings in a frightful situation  we never shot anything inside the pit  that wasn't an editorial decision that  we made afterwards that was just  something we said we would we  want to do their other exam and I'm not  saying you know you can't do that it's  just for me it wasn't possible to do I  didn't want to exploit the violence  dramatically too much if you look at  Angie Vytas katene for example there's a  massacre I don't know if anybody have  seen anybody has seen that if you  haven't you should it's it's really a  terrific film and and it has a massacre  at the end of it and that massacre is  shown in my new detail now you have to  know the backstory is that Vytas father  was shot in that massacre and his mom  lost her mind  subsequently to that to that murder so  so for vitai it was in a sense and  exorcism and he had to just approach  this an entirely different way you look  at a Rosselini movie he does it entirely  different again he'd never even shows  you know muzzle flashes because to him  that's too dramatic he just shows people  pulling triggers for for in some of his  World War 2 films so where do you hope  people think about despots differently  or how this story ties in to what's  happening not just here but across  Europe right now  I think yeah I mean it's unfortunately  happening everywhere and nothing has  been a greater surprise to me in my life  I thought we went you know past the dark  ages and we went through you know the  Enlightenment and Renaissance and who  would want to go back you know and we've  gone back and there's there a lot of  strong men who are who are using a very  very sharp nationalistic chauvinistic  Sanna phobic rhetoric to to my in  people's fears because it's a much  easier emotion to mine or to create than  anything rational based you exploit  their fear I think what we're witnessing  again and again you know here in this  country and in Poland and in the Czech  Republic and in all many other places is  that you have people who play lip  service to to the form of democracy but  what they're really doing at every turn  is they hollow it out and they  follow it out by ignoring the norms of  democracy and of course you know there  is no form without a norm and and and I  I think what we tend to forget and I  found us  I found this surprising you know after  9/11 when a lot of civil rights were  were you know disappeared that that  nobody seems to understand that  democracy is not a right it's a it's a  privilege and and I and I think you know  we have to be vigilant and it always  starts with rhetoric and that's  partially the little bit of analysis I  mean there's a there they're three steps  toward massacres that are always in  place no matter whether it's Africa or  Bosnia or Indonesia it doesn't matter or  Germany it always starts with rhetoric  it always starts with of them against us  rhetoric with a with a dehumanizing  rhetoric and the rhetoric always paves  the way for action there's no way you  can have this kind of sharp rhetoric in  the world without people taking action  and of course the people with the  rhetoric can say oh I didn't say that  you know but for example you know there  would be a speech during during you know  gnashing - there would be a Hitler  speech on the radio and five hundred  miles to the to the to the West somebody  would be listening to this speech and  nobody said please drag all the Jews out  of the houses and beat them up that was  not part of the speech but part of the  speech was and it was of course all code  as it is now you know we have to defend  the German blood we have to do this we  have to do that and of course the people  listening to it go and this is back to  the dynamic structure of National  Socialism people hearing it go okay we  know exactly what to do you know we're  gonna drag them all out and we're gonna  beat them up and and after you know in  in our cultural environment you have to  append all judeo-christian notions you  know all the things that you've been  taught  to be true or no longer true which I  can't even imagine what kind of whiplash  that must be and then everything that's  normally illegal becomes legal and and  the way to get there of course is  through lies and through threats and  through coercion but also because people  don't step in the way this is not you  know this movie is not a is not a  classical tragedy in the sense that you  know in the third act the kings heads  gonna come off there's nothing faded  about this story never was this this  Massacre happened because of every  choice no matter how small by the people  involved in the story no matter how  peripheral they all played a part and  when nobody says no you know terrible  things happen and I guess you know I I  mean I'm very I started this project  twelve years ago and at that point  people said to me friends who I would be  you know share it with said why would  you do that to yourself why would you do  that to us  and I and I maintained that it was going  to happen again and again because it's  human nature and because the membrane  between chaos and civilization is paper  thin and and sadly in the last four  years the film has become more relevant  on that happy note Robert thank you so  much for coming and thank you for  bringing your film to us
 

